  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP  Preventing and Responding to Violent Extremism in the Atlantic Corridor</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Countries in the Gulf of Guinea (Benin, C&#xF4;te d&#x2019;Ivoire, Ghana and Togo) have recently been confronted with the expansion of violent extremism, terrorism, transnational criminality, and other threats related to maritime piracy and drug trafficking, which are now threatening their stability. The increasing risk of the spread of extremist and terrorist groups from the Sahel (particularly from Burkina Faso and Mali) towards coastal states, raises fears of what is termed as a &#x201C;spill-over&#x201D; effect that could have serious consequences for the stability of these areas. This project seeks to a apply a complementary regional prevention approach in Benin, Togo, Ghana, Cote d&apos;Ivoire and Burkina Faso</narrative>
  </description>
